## Phase 2 — Backend Setup

### Step 3 — Install stripe/stripe-php

💡 **Best Practice:** This is the official Stripe PHP SDK — gives you direct access to
all Stripe API objects like `PaymentIntent`, `Webhook`, `Customer` etc.
When going live later, this gets replaced by `laravel/cashier` which wraps this same SDK.

```bash
composer require stripe/stripe-php
```

Verify it installed — you should see `stripe/stripe-php` in your `composer.json` require section.

### Step 4 — Create the Payment Controller

```bash
php artisan make:controller PaymentController
```

Open `app/Http/Controllers/PaymentController.php` and replace with:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\Order;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse;
use Stripe\Stripe;
use Stripe\PaymentIntent;
use Stripe\Exception\ApiErrorException;
use Inertia\Inertia;
use Inertia\Response;

class PaymentController extends Controller
{
    /**
     * Create a Stripe PaymentIntent and return the clientSecret to the frontend.
     *
     * The clientSecret is a temporary token that lets the frontend
     * confirm the payment using the card details — safely via Stripe.js.
     */
    public function createIntent(Request $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $request->validate([
            'order_id' => ['required', 'integer', 'exists:orders,id'],
        ]);

        $order = Order::findOrFail($request->order_id);

        // Always verify the order belongs to the authenticated user
        // Never let one user pay for another user's order
        if ($order->user_id !== auth()->id()) {
            abort(403, 'Unauthorized.');
        }

        // If a PaymentIntent already exists for this order, reuse it
        // This prevents duplicate charges if the user refreshes the page
        if ($order->transaction_id) {
            return response()->json([
                'clientSecret' => $order->transaction_id,
            ]);
        }

        try {
            // Set your secret key — this authenticates your request to Stripe
            Stripe::setApiKey(config('services.stripe.secret'));

            // Create the PaymentIntent on Stripe's servers
            // Amount must be in SMALLEST currency unit (cents for USD)
            // Example: $36.72 → 3672 cents
            $paymentIntent = PaymentIntent::create([
                'amount'   => (int) round($order->total * 100),
                'currency' => 'usd',

                // metadata lets you store your own data on the Stripe object
                // We use it to link the Stripe payment back to our order
                'metadata' => [
                    'order_id'     => $order->id,
                    'order_number' => $order->order_number,
                    'user_id'      => auth()->id(),
                ],

                // Automatically shows the best payment methods for the customer
                'automatic_payment_methods' => [
                    'enabled' => true,
                ],
            ]);

            // Save the PaymentIntent ID on our order record
            // We need this to look up the order when the webhook arrives
            $order->update(['transaction_id' => $paymentIntent->id]);

            return response()->json([
                'clientSecret' => $paymentIntent->client_secret,
            ]);

        } catch (ApiErrorException $e) {
            // Stripe API errors — log them and return a clean message to the user
            \Log::error('Stripe PaymentIntent creation failed', [
                'error'    => $e->getMessage(),
                'order_id' => $order->id,
            ]);

            return response()->json([
                'error' => 'Unable to initialise payment. Please try again.',
            ], 500);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Called by the frontend AFTER Stripe.js confirms the payment.
     * We verify the payment succeeded directly with Stripe, then update the order.
     *
     * IMPORTANT: Never trust what the frontend sends.
     * Always verify payment status by calling Stripe directly.
     */
    public function confirm(Request $request): Response
    {
        $request->validate([
            'payment_intent_id' => ['required', 'string'],
        ]);

        try {
            Stripe::setApiKey(config('services.stripe.secret'));

            // Retrieve the PaymentIntent from Stripe to check its real status
            $paymentIntent = PaymentIntent::retrieve(
                $request->payment_intent_id
            );

            // If payment did not succeed, send user back with an error
            if ($paymentIntent->status !== 'succeeded') {
                return Inertia::render('Checkout/Index', [
                    'error' => 'Payment was not successful. Please try again.',
                ]);
            }

            // Find the order using the metadata we stored when creating the intent
            $order = Order::findOrFail($paymentIntent->metadata->order_id);

            // Update order status
            $order->update([
                'payment_status' => 'paid',
                'status'         => 'processing',
                'paid_at'        => now(),
            ]);

            return Inertia::render('Checkout/Confirmation', [
                'order' => $order->load('items.product'),
            ]);

        } catch (ApiErrorException $e) {
            \Log::error('Stripe payment confirmation failed', [
                'error'              => $e->getMessage(),
                'payment_intent_id'  => $request->payment_intent_id,
            ]);

            return Inertia::render('Checkout/Index', [
                'error' => 'Something went wrong. Please contact support.',
            ]);
        }
    }
}
```

💡 **Why two steps (createIntent + confirm)?**
- `createIntent` → runs when the checkout page loads. Creates the payment on Stripe's side.
- `confirm` → runs after the user enters their card and Stripe processes it.
This two-step flow is the **Payment Intents API** — Stripe's recommended approach for web payments.

### Step 5 — Create the Webhook Controller

```bash
php artisan make:controller WebhookController
```

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\Order;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\Response;
use Stripe\Webhook;
use Stripe\Exception\SignatureVerificationException;

class WebhookController extends Controller
{
    /**
     * Handle incoming webhook events from Stripe.
     *
     * Stripe calls this endpoint automatically when payment events happen —
     * even if the user closes their browser before reaching the confirmation page.
     * This is why webhooks are essential: they guarantee our order gets updated.
     */
    public function handle(Request $request): Response
    {
        $payload   = $request->getContent();
        $sigHeader = $request->header('Stripe-Signature');
        $secret    = config('services.stripe.webhook_secret');

        // Verify the request actually came from Stripe
        // Stripe signs every webhook with your webhook secret
        // If someone sends a fake request to this URL, this check will reject it
        try {
            $event = Webhook::constructEvent($payload, $sigHeader, $secret);

        } catch (SignatureVerificationException $e) {
            \Log::warning('Stripe webhook signature verification failed', [
                'error' => $e->getMessage(),
            ]);

            // Return 400 to tell Stripe something went wrong
            return response('Invalid signature.', 400);
        }

        // Route the event to the correct handler method
        // We use match() — cleaner than a long if/elseif chain
        match ($event->type) {
            'payment_intent.succeeded'      => $this->onPaymentSucceeded($event),
            'payment_intent.payment_failed' => $this->onPaymentFailed($event),
            default                         => null,  // safely ignore all other events
        };

        // Always return 200 — tells Stripe "I received this, thank you"
        // If you return anything else, Stripe will retry the webhook
        return response('Webhook handled.', 200);
    }

    /**
     * Payment succeeded — mark the order as paid.
     */
    private function onPaymentSucceeded(object $event): void
    {
        $paymentIntent = $event->data->object;
        $orderId       = $paymentIntent->metadata->order_id ?? null;

        if (! $orderId) {
            \Log::warning('Webhook: payment_intent.succeeded — no order_id in metadata');
            return;
        }

        $order = Order::find($orderId);

        if (! $order) {
            \Log::warning('Webhook: order not found', ['order_id' => $orderId]);
            return;
        }

        // Only update if not already paid — webhooks can sometimes fire twice
        if ($order->payment_status !== 'paid') {
            $order->update([
                'payment_status' => 'paid',
                'status'         => 'processing',
                'paid_at'        => now(),
            ]);

            \Log::info('Order marked as paid via webhook', [
                'order_id'          => $orderId,
                'payment_intent_id' => $paymentIntent->id,
            ]);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Payment failed — keep the order as pending so the user can retry.
     */
    private function onPaymentFailed(object $event): void
    {
        $paymentIntent = $event->data->object;
        $orderId       = $paymentIntent->metadata->order_id ?? null;

        if (! $orderId) return;

        Order::find($orderId)?->update([
            'payment_status' => 'pending',
            'status'         => 'pending',
        ]);

        \Log::info('Payment failed for order', ['order_id' => $orderId]);
    }
}
```

### Step 6 — Add Routes

Open `routes/web.php`:

```php
use App\Http\Controllers\PaymentController;
use App\Http\Controllers\WebhookController;

// Payment routes — authenticated users only
Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    // ... your existing auth routes ...

    Route::post('/payment/intent', [PaymentController::class, 'createIntent'])
        ->name('payment.intent');

    Route::post('/payment/confirm', [PaymentController::class, 'confirm'])
        ->name('payment.confirm');
});

// Stripe Webhook — OUTSIDE auth middleware
// Stripe calls this directly — it has no session or CSRF token
Route::post('/stripe/webhook', [WebhookController::class, 'handle'])
    ->name('stripe.webhook');
```

### Step 7 — Exclude Webhook from CSRF

Stripe cannot send a CSRF token with its requests — we must whitelist the webhook URL.

Open `bootstrap/app.php`:

```php
->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
    $middleware->validateCsrfTokens(except: [
        'stripe/webhook',   // Stripe webhook is secured by signature instead
    ]);

    $middleware->alias([
        'admin'       => \App\Http\Middleware\AdminMiddleware::class,
        'super_admin' => \App\Http\Middleware\SuperAdminMiddleware::class,
    ]);
})
```

🔒 **Security Note:** We are NOT disabling CSRF for the whole app — only this one URL.
The webhook is protected by Stripe's signature verification inside `WebhookController`
instead of CSRF. This is the correct pattern.

