## Phase 4 — Local Webhook Testing

### Step 12 — Install Stripe CLI

The Stripe CLI forwards webhook events from Stripe to your local `localhost` during development.

**macOS:**
```bash
brew install stripe/stripe-cli/stripe
```

**Windows:** Download from [https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-cli](https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-cli)

**Linux:**
```bash
curl -s https://packages.stripe.dev/api/security/keypair/stripe-cli-gpg/public \
  | gpg --dearmor \
  | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/stripe.gpg

echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/stripe.gpg] \
  https://packages.stripe.dev/stripe-cli-debian-local stable main" \
  | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/stripe.list

sudo apt update && sudo apt install stripe
```

### Step 13 — Login and Forward Webhooks

```bash
# Login to Stripe (opens browser for authentication)
stripe login

# Start forwarding webhooks to your local server
stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8000/stripe/webhook
```

The CLI outputs a webhook signing secret:
```
> Ready! Your webhook signing secret is whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx (^C to quit)
```

Copy that value and update your `.env`:
```env
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
```

Then restart your Laravel server so it picks up the new env value:
```bash
php artisan config:clear
php artisan serve
```

