## What You Learned in This Integration

```
PaymentIntent      The Stripe object representing one payment attempt.
                   Created server-side, confirmed client-side.

clientSecret       A temporary one-time token Stripe gives you.
                   Safe to send to frontend — useless without the actual card.

Stripe Elements    Pre-built secure card input rendered in an iframe by Stripe.
                   You never handle raw card numbers — auto PCI compliant.

Webhooks           Stripe calling YOUR server to notify you of payment events.
                   More reliable than frontend redirects — fires even if browser closes.

metadata           Custom data you attach to a Stripe object (like order_id).
                   Used to link Stripe payments back to your database records.

Test Mode          A completely separate Stripe environment.
                   All pk_test_ / sk_test_ keys only work here — no real money.
```

---

## When Going Live — Migration to Laravel Cashier

When you are ready to launch, follow these steps to replace the raw SDK with Cashier:

```bash
# Step 1 — Remove raw SDK
composer remove stripe/stripe-php

# Step 2 — Install Cashier
composer require laravel/cashier
php artisan vendor:publish --tag="cashier-migrations"
php artisan migrate

# Step 3 — Add Billable trait to User model
# (see stripe-integration-guide-cashier.md — to be created at that time)
```

**What changes in code:**
```
PaymentController  → rewrite using $user->createPaymentIntent()
WebhookController  → replace with Cashier's built-in webhook handler
Stripe::setApiKey() calls → removed (Cashier handles automatically)
```

**What stays the same:**
```
✅ All Stripe concepts (PaymentIntent, webhooks, metadata)
✅ StripePayment.vue frontend component — no changes needed
✅ API keys — same keys work with Cashier
✅ Webhook routes and CSRF exclusion
✅ Test cards and Stripe CLI workflow
```

